# ProductOS local-only state
productos/.local/

# Running `productos init` in this repo root scaffolds a corpus. That is derived state —
# anyone who runs init gets it — so it is not committed. The leading slash matters: the
# worked example at docs/example/productos/ IS committed.
/productos/

# The worked-example corpus is committed; its scratch install is not
docs/example/.claude/
docs/example/.git/
docs/example/productos/.local/

# The v2 working corpus is regenerated from v2-seed/ by `productos v2 reset` before every
# review run, so committing it would only ever record whichever run finished last.
# v2-seed/ IS committed — it is the pristine copy every run starts from.
/v2/

# Database dumps from `make backup` — a customer roadmap, in one file.
/backups/

# ⛔ ROOTED, NOT BARE — AND DELIBERATELY BELT-AND-BRACES WITH /backups/ ABOVE.
#
# `make backup-remote` writes a full dump of the managed store, and that store holds a real
# client's product truth. BACKUP_DIR is overridable and `pg_dump > something.sql` in the repo root
# is one keystroke away, so ignoring only the directory leaves the obvious mistake available. The
# leading slash is what keeps drizzle/*.sql — the migrations, which MUST be tracked — out of this.
#
# Lifted from bilrost-workers/.gitignore, which rooted its own pattern after review found two 95MB
# dumps untracked in a working tree, one `git add -A` away from being permanent in history.
/*.sql
/*.sql.gz
/*.dump

# `productos init claude` registers the MCP server here, and for a dev install the command is an
# ABSOLUTE path to the checkout it was run from — so this checkout's copy names one machine's
# worktree. Committing it hands everybody else a path that does not exist, and from a worktree it
# would quietly re-aim the main checkout's sessions. Derived state, like /productos/ above: run
# `productos init claude --update` and you have it.
/.mcp.json
